## Implementation Plan: Responsive TS Fixes and Three.js Optimization

---

### Section 1: iOS visualViewport Fix

**Goal:** Replace `window.innerHeight` in `Device.ts` with `visualViewport.height` on iOS, and add a `visualViewport` resize listener in `ResizeSetup.ts` alongside the existing `window.resize` listener.

---

**File: `/c/Users/edn_y/renew_nineonelabs/resource/ts/utils/Device.ts`**

Lines 22-26 — the `updateFromWindow` method.

Current logic (lines 24-25):
```
this._viewportWidth = window.innerWidth ?? 0;
this._viewportHeight = window.innerHeight ?? 0;
```

Change to:
```
this._viewportWidth = window.innerWidth ?? 0;
this._viewportHeight = _resolveViewportHeight();
```

Add a module-level helper function above the `Device` class (after the `detectDeviceType` function):

```ts
function _resolveViewportHeight(): number {
  if (
    typeof window !== "undefined" &&
    window.visualViewport != null &&
    /iPhone|iPod|iPad/i.test(UA) ||
    (/Macintosh/i.test(UA) && hasTouch)
  ) {
    return window.visualViewport.height;
  }
  return window.innerHeight ?? 0;
}
```

Why: On iOS, `window.innerHeight` does not track the visible area accurately when the Safari address bar animates in or out. `window.visualViewport.height` reflects the actual rendered viewport height. The guard ensures the fallback applies only on iOS-detected devices so non-iOS behavior is unchanged.

---

**File: `/c/Users/edn_y/renew_nineonelabs/resource/ts/app/ResizeSetup.ts`**

Lines 14, 35-47, 62-63 — event binding and unbinding.

Current state: one `window.addEventListener("resize", this._boundHandler)` at line 47, removed at line 62.

Changes needed:

1. Add a new private field at line 14 area:
   ```ts
   private _visualViewportHandler: (() => void) | null = null;
   ```

2. In `startListening`, after the existing `window.addEventListener("resize", ...)` at line 47, add:
   ```ts
   if (typeof window !== "undefined" && window.visualViewport != null) {
     this._visualViewportHandler = this._boundHandler;
     window.visualViewport.addEventListener("resize", this._visualViewportHandler);
   }
   ```

3. In `stopListening`, after `window.removeEventListener("resize", ...)` at line 62, add:
   ```ts
   if (this._visualViewportHandler && window.visualViewport != null) {
     window.visualViewport.removeEventListener("resize", this._visualViewportHandler);
     this._visualViewportHandler = null;
   }
   ```

Why: `window.resize` fires unreliably for Safari address bar hide/show transitions. `visualViewport.resize` is the correct event for that. Reusing `_boundHandler` (already debounced at 250ms) means no new debounce instance is needed and no double-firing risk on non-iOS (where `_visualViewportHandler` is null). The symmetry in `stopListening` prevents a dangling listener.

---

### Section 2: `window.innerHeight` Cleanup — 4 Files

**Goal:** Replace direct `window.innerHeight` reads with `ResponsiveRuntime.getDefault().getSnapshot().viewportHeight`. This ensures all height reads flow through the same responsive pipeline that already applies the `visualViewport` fix from Section 1.

---

**File 1: `/c/Users/edn_y/renew_nineonelabs/resource/ts/runtime/PinnedRevealRuntime.ts`**

Line 93 — inside `_resolveEnd`.

Current:
```ts
return () => `+=${Math.max(section.offsetHeight - window.innerHeight, 0)}`;
```

Change to:
```ts
return () => {
  const vh = ResponsiveRuntime.getDefault().getSnapshot().viewportHeight;
  return `+=${Math.max(section.offsetHeight - vh, 0)}`;
};
```

Also add the import at the top of the file (it is not currently imported here):
```ts
import { ResponsiveRuntime } from "./ResponsiveRuntime.js";
```

Why: This is the GSAP ScrollTrigger `end` resolver. It runs at scroll-trigger refresh time, meaning it captures the live viewport height at that moment. Using the snapshot keeps it consistent with whatever height the responsive system recorded most recently. On iOS, where the address bar causes the raw `window.innerHeight` to jump on scroll, using the snapshot (which is updated via the debounced resize handler) gives a stable, intentional value rather than an instantaneous transient one.

---

**File 2: `/c/Users/edn_y/renew_nineonelabs/resource/ts/runtime/SubpageStarfieldRuntime.ts`**

Line 125 — inside `_syncScrollBoost`.

Current:
```ts
const viewportHeight = Math.max(window.innerHeight || 0, 1);
```

Change to:
```ts
const viewportHeight = Math.max(
  ResponsiveRuntime.getDefault().getSnapshot().viewportHeight || 0,
  1
);
```

`ResponsiveRuntime` is already imported at line 3, so no import change is needed.

Why: `_syncScrollBoost` runs on every `scroll` event, which is extremely frequent. Using the snapshot avoids a direct DOM read on every scroll tick. The scroll boost normalization divides `delta` (pixels scrolled) by `viewportHeight`, so on iOS this value can oscillate if `window.innerHeight` returns different values mid-scroll due to address bar animation — using the snapshot gives a stable denominator.

---

**File 3: `/c/Users/edn_y/renew_nineonelabs/resource/ts/pages/works/runtime/WorksPageRuntime.ts`**

Line 524 — inside `_isThresholdReached`.

Current:
```ts
rect.bottom - window.innerHeight <= WORK_GRID_INFINITE_CONFIG.thresholdPx
```

Change to:
```ts
const vh = ResponsiveRuntime.getDefault().getSnapshot().viewportHeight;
rect.bottom - vh <= WORK_GRID_INFINITE_CONFIG.thresholdPx
```

Add the import if not already present. Check with:
```
grep -n "ResponsiveRuntime" resource/ts/pages/works/runtime/WorksPageRuntime.ts
```
If absent, add `import { ResponsiveRuntime } from "../../../runtime/ResponsiveRuntime.js";`.

Why: Infinite scroll threshold logic runs on every scroll event. On iOS Safari, when the address bar hides as the user scrolls down, `window.innerHeight` temporarily grows, which can cause `rect.bottom - window.innerHeight` to shrink and falsely trigger an early load. Using the snapshot avoids this false trigger.

---

**File 4: `/c/Users/edn_y/renew_nineonelabs/resource/ts/utils/PerformanceProfile.ts`**

Lines 115-116 — inside `getViewportInfo`.

Current:
```ts
const width = window.innerWidth ?? 1440;
const height = window.innerHeight ?? 900;
```

Change to:
```ts
const width = window.innerWidth ?? 1440;
const height =
  (typeof window !== "undefined" && window.visualViewport != null
    ? window.visualViewport.height
    : window.innerHeight) ?? 900;
```

Why: `getViewportInfo` is called by `getDefaultEnvironment`, which is the fallback when `getMainProfile` is invoked without an explicit `ResponsiveEnvironmentInput`. This initial profiling at boot determines the performance tier (`low`/`mid`/`high`) and thus affects things like `enginePixelRatioMax` and bloom settings. On iOS, if the boot snapshot sees a deflated `innerHeight` (because the address bar is still visible), the `isCompact` flag (line 121: `width <= 1024 || height <= 768`) could incorrectly classify the device as compact and downgrade the tier. Using `visualViewport.height` here matches the same source of truth that `Device.ts` will now use after the Section 1 fix.

---

### Section 3: SCSS `100vh` to `100dvh` Migration

**Pattern (mirrors `_drawer.scss` lines 14-17):**

For each replacement, declare the `100vh` version first as a fallback, then immediately re-declare the same property with `100dvh`. Browsers that support `dvh` override the first declaration; older browsers silently ignore the second. Do NOT use `@supports (height: 100dvh)` — simply stack the two declarations. This is the same pattern already used in `_drawer.scss` (two `height:` declarations in sequence) and `_sub.scss:1352-1353`.

For values that combine with `calc()` (e.g. `calc(100vh - var(--header-height))`), the pattern becomes:
```scss
min-height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height));
min-height: calc(100dvh - var(--header-height));
```

For bare `100vh`/`height: 100vh` pairs, the pattern is:
```scss
min-height: 100vh;
min-height: 100dvh;
height: 100vh;
height: 100dvh;
```

---

**File: `/c/Users/edn_y/renew_nineonelabs/resource/scss/pages/_works-canvas.scss`**

Line 52 — `min-height: 100vh` inside `&[data-view="grid"]`:
```scss
// before
min-height: 100vh;

// after
min-height: 100vh;
min-height: 100dvh;
```

Line 350 — `min-height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height))` inside `&__grid`:
```scss
// before
min-height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height));

// after
min-height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height));
min-height: calc(100dvh - var(--header-height));
```

Why: The works canvas in `data-view="grid"` is a full-page scroll container. On iOS, if the address bar is visible when the page loads, `100vh` is calculated against the smaller viewport. When the bar hides on scroll, the element's height does not update and a visual gap appears at the bottom.

---

**File: `/c/Users/edn_y/renew_nineonelabs/resource/scss/pages/_sub.scss`**

Skip lines 106 (`100svh` — already a static-viewport unit, intentionally different) and lines 1352-1353 (already has `100dvh`).

Line 62-63 — `body.is-contact .scroll-container, #main, #main .main-content`:
```scss
// before
min-height: 100vh;
height: 100vh;

// after
min-height: 100vh;
min-height: 100dvh;
height: 100vh;
height: 100dvh;
```

Line 98 — `.no-main-contact[data-scene="contact"] .no-container-2xl`:
```scss
// before
min-height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height));

// after
min-height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height));
min-height: calc(100dvh - var(--header-height));
```

Line 129 — `.no-sub-about-hero-intro__inner`:
```scss
// before
height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height));

// after
height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height));
height: calc(100dvh - var(--header-height));
```

Line 251 — `.no-sub-about-vision__txt` (sticky `min-height`):
```scss
// before
min-height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height));

// after
min-height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height));
min-height: calc(100dvh - var(--header-height));
```

Line 300 — `.no-sub-about-vision__txt` inside `@include mq(md)`:
```scss
// before
min-height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height));

// after
min-height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height));
min-height: calc(100dvh - var(--header-height));
```

Line 326 — `.no-sub-about-who__stage`:
```scss
// before
min-height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height));

// after
min-height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height));
min-height: calc(100dvh - var(--header-height));
```

Why for sticky elements specifically: Lines 251, 300, and 326 are `position: sticky` elements used as scroll-scrub anchors. If their `min-height` is set against a stale `100vh` (the value measured before the address bar hid), the sticky duration will be shorter than intended, causing scroll-linked animations to end too early on iOS.

---

**File: `/c/Users/edn_y/renew_nineonelabs/resource/scss/base/_utils.scss`**

Lines 22-23 — `html[data-page-mode="orbit"] .scroll-container, #main.no-sub-about`:
```scss
// before
min-height: 100vh;
height: 100vh;

// after
min-height: 100vh;
min-height: 100dvh;
height: 100vh;
height: 100dvh;
```

Lines 67-68 — same pattern in a second `html[data-*]` author-mode block (scroll-container/#main):
```scss
min-height: 100vh; → min-height: 100vh; min-height: 100dvh;
height: 100vh;     → height: 100vh; height: 100dvh;
```

Lines 105-106 — third author-mode block:
Same transformation as above.

Lines 157-158 — fourth author-mode block (`html[data-works-mode="author"]`):
Same transformation.

Why: These author-mode utility overrides lock the scroll container to full-screen height. On iOS, without `dvh`, the container height does not dynamically match the visual viewport when the address bar toggles, causing the Three.js canvas to not fill the screen correctly during preview/author sessions.

---

### Section 4: Three.js Scatter Optimization

**File: `/c/Users/edn_y/renew_nineonelabs/resource/ts/pages/works/render/WorksSceneManager.ts`**

Lines 923-926 — inside the `onResize` closure within `bindEvents`.

Current:
```ts
const resizeScaleBase = viewport.width < 768 ? CARD_SCALE_MOBILE_BASE_WIDTH : CARD_SCALE_BASE_WIDTH;
this._cardScale = Math.min(1, viewport.width / resizeScaleBase);
this._rebuildScatterPositions();
this.applyCategory(this.activeCategory);
```

Change to:
```ts
const resizeScaleBase = viewport.width < 768 ? CARD_SCALE_MOBILE_BASE_WIDTH : CARD_SCALE_BASE_WIDTH;
const nextCardScale = Math.min(1, viewport.width / resizeScaleBase);
if (nextCardScale !== this._cardScale) {
  this._cardScale = nextCardScale;
  this._rebuildScatterPositions();
}
this.applyCategory(this.activeCategory);
```

Why: `_rebuildScatterPositions` calls `buildScatterPositions`, which does `Math.ceil(Math.sqrt(...))`, array allocation, and `sortScatterPositionsFromCenter` (a sort pass) on every resize event — even when the viewport width hasn't crossed any breakpoint boundary that would change `_cardScale`. On desktop, resizing the browser height (common, e.g., collapsing the DevTools panel) triggers the resize handler with an identical `viewport.width`, computing the same `_cardScale` value. The early-exit guard short-circuits the expensive rebuild in that case. `applyCategory` must still run on every resize because it repositions meshes relative to camera frustum dimensions, not `_cardScale` directly. The guard is a floating-point equality check on a value computed from `viewport.width` via `Math.min(1, width / constant)`, so identical widths produce bit-for-bit identical float results — the guard is reliable.

---

