Lines 67-68 — same pattern in a second `html[data-*]` author-mode block (scroll-container/#main):
```scss
min-height: 100vh; → min-height: 100vh; min-height: 100dvh;
height: 100vh;     → height: 100vh; height: 100dvh;
```

Lines 105-106 — third author-mode block:
Same transformation as above.

Lines 157-158 — fourth author-mode block (`html[data-works-mode="author"]`):
Same transformation.

Why: These author-mode utility overrides lock the scroll container to full-screen height. On iOS, without `dvh`, the container height does not dynamically match the visual viewport when the address bar toggles, causing the Three.js canvas to not fill the screen correctly during preview/author sessions.

---

### Section 4: Three.js Scatter Optimization

**File: `/c/Users/edn_y/renew_nineonelabs/resource/ts/pages/works/render/WorksSceneManager.ts`**

Lines 923-926 — inside the `onResize` closure within `bindEvents`.

Current:
```ts
const resizeScaleBase = viewport.width < 768 ? CARD_SCALE_MOBILE_BASE_WIDTH : CARD_SCALE_BASE_WIDTH;
this._cardScale = Math.min(1, viewport.width / resizeScaleBase);
this._rebuildScatterPositions();
this.applyCategory(this.activeCategory);
```

Change to:
```ts
const resizeScaleBase = viewport.width < 768 ? CARD_SCALE_MOBILE_BASE_WIDTH : CARD_SCALE_BASE_WIDTH;
const nextCardScale = Math.min(1, viewport.width / resizeScaleBase);
if (nextCardScale !== this._cardScale) {
  this._cardScale = nextCardScale;
  this._rebuildScatterPositions();
}
this.applyCategory(this.activeCategory);
```

Why: `_rebuildScatterPositions` calls `buildScatterPositions`, which does `Math.ceil(Math.sqrt(...))`, array allocation, and `sortScatterPositionsFromCenter` (a sort pass) on every resize event — even when the viewport width hasn't crossed any breakpoint boundary that would change `_cardScale`. On desktop, resizing the browser height (common, e.g., collapsing the DevTools panel) triggers the resize handler with an identical `viewport.width`, computing the same `_cardScale` value. The early-exit guard short-circuits the expensive rebuild in that case. `applyCategory` must still run on every resize because it repositions meshes relative to camera frustum dimensions, not `_cardScale` directly. The guard is a floating-point equality check on a value computed from `viewport.width` via `Math.min(1, width / constant)`, so identical widths produce bit-for-bit identical float results — the guard is reliable.

---

